r": -8.0, "points_lost": -0.33, "best": "D9"}], "review": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "title": "The Wall That Walked Away", "summary": "The 14k won by 8 points with White's komi of 7. The game was close until the middle, when the 14k's G8 (5.3 points lost) was answered by Opus 5.5's H6, which lost 5.3 points itself and wiped out the gain. Later, trouble on the left side and a lost fight around E3 and E4 pushed Black's deficit to about 8 points, which the endgame never closed. Neither side made a blunder: Opus 5.5 averaged 1.3 points lost per move and the 14k 1.0.", "moments": [{"turn": 4, "text": "The 14k's C7 lost 2.8 points, with the engine preferring E3, but it still moved the lead from -2.4 to +0.4 in Black's favor. It was an early sign that the game would be a swingy one."}, {"turn": 8, "text": "The 14k's G8 was its worst move, losing 5.3 points and moving the lead from -1.6 to +3.7 for Black. After H7 pushed in from the right, the engine wanted F7, and G8 left Black with a chance to profit."}, {"turn": 9, "text": "Opus 5.5 did not take that chance. H6 lost 5.3 points, its worst move of the game, and the lead dropped from +3.7 straight back to -1.6. The engine's choice was F6, the point White took on the very next move."}, {"turn": 15, "text": "E3 lost 2.9 points and took the lead from -4.4 to -7.3, with the engine preferring B3. White's E4 answered, and the two stones sit side by side in the final bottom-left."}, {"turn": 16, "text": "White's E4 lost 4.0 points, with the engine choosing B3, and the lead recovered from -7.3 to -3.3 for Black. It was the one time the 14k gave a big chunk back late, but Black's F4 then lost 2.6 points and the gap grew again to -5.9."}, {"turn": 31, "text": "Opus 5.5's F5 lost 1.3 points, taking the lead from -7.9 to -9.2, the lowest of the game for Black. The 14k's G9 gave back 1.3 points, and the game settled into a gap of roughly 8 points that neither side changed before the double pass."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py and after.py parse a pasted board and print group liberties, with after.py applying one named move and removing captures, while count.py tallies territory on a given board with named dead stones. None of them loops over candidate moves or plays out continuations, so all of it works on the current position.", "stop": false}}